course: dictionary
lesson: 4127
title: Gleaning: picking up what is left
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Glean is a verb with two meanings. The first is to collect information bit by bit, often with effort: «I gleaned a few facts from the leaflet.» You did not get everything at once. You picked up small pieces, one by one.
explain: The second meaning comes from farming. When the harvesters had finished, some grain was left lying in the field. To glean was to gather that leftover grain. The example «In Israel Ruth gleaned, gathering the leftover grain in the field of Boaz» shows this meaning.
explain: Use context to check which meaning fits. Ask: is the sentence about a field and grain, or about facts and information? Then swap in "collected bit by bit" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
fact: Glean means to gather something bit by bit, often with effort.
fact: «I gleaned a few facts from the leaflet» means I collected some facts from it, one by one.
fact: In the Bible, farmers were told to leave the edges of the field so the poor could glean there.
fact: Ruth gleaned in the field of Boaz, and he gave her extra grain and later married her.
fact: Useful phrases: «glean information from», «glean facts», «glean in the fields».
exercise: «I gleaned a few facts from the leaflet.» What does gleaned mean here? | Read it aloud to a friend | Ran fast across the field | *Collected bit by bit, with effort | Threw the leaflet away
exercise: In Ruth's story, what did she glean? | Stories about the sea | A new pair of shoes | Gold coins from a king | *Leftover grain from a field after the harvest
exercise: «Boaz protected her, gave her extra grain and later married her.» Find and copy one word that means kept safe. | gave | extra | *protected | married
test: «Our class gleaned the key dates from the history website.» What does gleaned suggest? | Threw the books away | Ran around the field | Ate a big lunch in the playground | *Found out information bit by bit
test: Why were farmers in the Bible told to leave the edges of the field? | So the harvesters could rest | So the sheep could graze | *So the poor could glean there | So the field would be empty
exam: Which phrase means finding information bit by bit? | glean in the fields | leave the edges | harvest the sea | *glean facts from
